  "query_preview": "..\n\nEarly morning upper air analysis shows broad lower amplitude upper \nridging anchored locally, with a loosely organized southern stream \nmid level wave ejecting into the midwest. This wave is forecast to \nlift across southeast Michigan over the next 24 hours. The existing \nelevated height field within the background low level southeast flow \nwill maintain a milder resident thermal profile for the first day of \nNovember, with a noted period of warm air advection across the 850-\n925 mb layer as the lead edge of the wave encroaches. Thick canopy \nof mid level cloud will cap the insolation potential, but the \nadvective process will aid in lifting temperatures into the middle \nand upper 60s most locations. While a spotty shower or two remains \nplausible as modest height falls work through, lack of meaningful \nmoisture quality across the lowest 6-7k ft favors mainly dry \nconditions to finish the weekend period. \n\nDynamic mid level wave now seen on water vapor lifting into the west \ncoast will strengthen while closing as it enters the plains by early \nnext week. A notable increase in the upper height field immediately \ndownstream will maintain this stretch of warmer conditions locally, \nwith 850 mb temperatures peaking in the lower teens Tuesday. This \nwill translate into highs of low to mid 70s by Tuesday - or a solid \n15 to 20 degrees above average for early November. Greater forced \nascent tied to more meaningful moist isentropic ascent will largely \nremain to the west thru this time, but with some suggestion that a \nwindow may exist for light shower production sometime Monday night \ninto early Tuesday with greater potential with northward extent. \n\nCentral conus closed low will slowly track east-northeast thru the \nlatter half of the week while gradually weakening with time. \nAttendant cold front currently projected to migrate through lower \nMichigan Wednesday and Wednesday night. Favorable environment will \nexist given the increase in moisture along the advancing front under \nstrengthening low level southwest flow for a high coverage of rain \nto develop during this time. Area will reside on the north flank of \nbetter instability residing across the Ohio valley, worthy of \nperhaps an isolated mention for a rumble of thunder. A late day \narrival will again lend favorably to continued very mild conditions, \nwith highs again forecast near 70 degrees Wednesday. Trending cooler \nwith potential for unsettled conditions to linger for the end of the \nweek as the trailing upper trough maintains moist cyclonic flow and \nsteeper mid level lapse rates. With that, temperatures still \nprojected to reside on the warmer side of average heading into next \nweekend.",
